  Iteration 1 declared tier 1: single mechanism, single knob, treatment vs control
  Justification: iter 1, single mechanism (preemption token accounting), single fix, treatment vs control
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether BLIS double-counts output tokens when KV-cache pressure causes request preemptions, inflating total_output_tokens by ~9.1%. The proposed fix subtracts already-counted decode tokens at preemption time so they are only tallied once (after the final reschedule). Two experiment arms compare a small-cache (preemption-heavy) run against a large-cache (no-preemption) run, before and after the fix.

  * Bug mechanism: In sim/simulator.go:647, TotalOutputTokens++ fires for every decode token, but when a request is preempted (batch_formation.go:232) and ProgressIndex resets to 0, those already-counted tokens are never subtracted — causing re-generation to count them again.
  * h-main prediction: After the fix, total_output_tokens with 600 KV blocks (high preemption) should equal the 60000-block (no preemption) baseline of 105,082 for the canonical workload (--rate 20, --num-requests 200, --seed 42). Before the fix, the small-cache run reports 114,630 — a 9.1% inflation.
  * h-control-negative prediction: With 60000 KV blocks (no preemptions), the fix produces byte-identical output to unfixed code, confirming the change is a no-op when no preemptions occur.
  * Code changes span three files: batch_formation.go (compute OutputTokensLost and clear ITL on preemption), simulator.go (subtract OutputTokensLost from TotalOutputTokens in scheduleBatch), and simulator_test.go (regression test asserting equal TotalOutputTokens across cache sizes).
  * Confirmation criterion: total_output_tokens matches exactly (105,082) across both cache configurations post-fix. Refutation: any remaining discrepancy would indicate additional double-counting paths or an incorrect OutputTokensLost calculation.
  * Design is minimal (Tier 1): single mechanism, single fix, two arms — appropriate for iteration 1.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

