────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether the iteration-1 fix for double-counting output tokens on preemption holds robustly across 5 random seeds and 3 KV cache pressure levels (600, 6000, and 60000 blocks). The core invariant is that total_output_tokens must be identical regardless of how many preemptions occur, meaning the fix correctly subtracts OutputTokensLost and clears ITL on every preemption event.

  * h-main: 5 seeds × 2 cache sizes (600 blocks = high preemption vs 60000 blocks = zero preemption). Prediction: total_output_tokens matches exactly across all 5 seeds with zero deviation. Any mismatch falsifies the fix.
  * h-control-negative: 5 seeds at 6000 blocks (moderate preemption, ~50-90 preemptions) vs 60000 blocks. This regime exercises both prefill-stage preemptions (OutputTokensLost=0) and decode-stage preemptions (OutputTokensLost>0), validating both branches of the fix.
  * Code changes span 3 files: batch_formation.go (compute OutputTokensLost before resetting ProgressIndex, clear ITL), simulator.go (subtract OutputTokensLost from TotalOutputTokens), and simulator_test.go (regression test). Total of 30 runs (5 seeds × 3 cache levels × 2 comparisons), each ~3 seconds.
  * Confirmation criteria: all 15 comparisons (5 seeds × 3 cache levels) show exact equality of total_output_tokens vs the no-preemption baseline. Any single mismatch triggers the diagnostic path (inspect specific seed/request for uncovered code paths or integer overflow).
  * This is a tier-2 escalation from iter-1, which confirmed the mechanism with a single seed. The experiment design is clean: same model (qwen3-14b), rate (20 req/s), and workload (200 requests) across all runs, varying only KV cache size and seed.

